# repo-map.example — copy to `repo-map` (beside this file, or beside your spira.conf) and
# replace every row with your own. THIS FILE IS EXAMPLES. The rows below name repositories
# that do not exist; the harness reads `repo-map` if it is present and falls back to this
# file only so that a clean clone resolves to something rather than to nothing.
#
# `repo:<name>` to the working copy an aeon may edit, the ref its work is cut from and lands
# on, how it lands, the formatter its machine-produced trees are put back through, and the
# gate it must pass.
#
# THE REPOSITORY COMES FROM THE BEAD, NOT FROM THE FAYTH. A fayth supplies the persona, the
# statutes and the tool allowlist; the bead supplies the workspace, via a `repo:<name>` label.
# That distinction is what makes cross-repository work possible at all: while the repository
# was a constant per persona, every fayth pointed at one checkout, so a bead for any other
# repository had no aeon that could open the file.
#
# Format:  name | path | land | base | format | gate | lanes
#
#   name   the value after `repo:` on a bead. It is a LABEL, not a directory: a repository
#          whose label and directory differ is exactly why this file exists rather than a
#          `<workspaces>/$name` guess.
#   path   the checkout an aeon gets a worktree against. Never a production checkout —
#          merging is not deploying (law-prod-is-a-different-checkout).
#   land   what the landing check does with a branch that passes its gate:
#            push   merge into `base` and push it. The branch IS the release.
#            pr     push the branch, open a pull request against `base`, arm auto-merge, and
#                   let the repository's own CI be the authority
#                   (law-green-prs-merge-themselves).
#            hold   gate it, note the bead once, and leave the branch for a human. For a
#                   repository this harness has no business advancing — one whose checkout is
#                   owned by something else, where rewriting a branch under someone's working
#                   tree is not ours to do.
#            queue  certify the branch via the gate and record it CERTIFIED for the batch
#                   builder. The batch builder (batch.sh) collects certified branches into one
#                   pull request; CI runs the full corpus on it; a green batch fast-forwards
#                   onto the base branch; every landed batch cuts a release. Base branch
#                   protection is required — run `queue.sh protect` after switching. A bead in
#                   queue mode must NOT park on CI: the batch PR is opened by the batch
#                   builder, not by the individual branch landing, and there is no run for a
#                   bead to wait for.
#
#          THIS COLUMN ALSO DECIDES WHETHER THERE IS A CI RUN TO WAIT FOR. Only `pr` opens a
#          pull request, so only `pr` has one — under `push`, `hold`, and `queue` the landing
#          gate is the whole gate for the individual branch, and a bead parked on CI in any of
#          those waits for an event that cannot occur. Because the park label is excluded from
#          every persona's predicate AND from the stranded-work report, that wait is invisible
#          as well as endless. The sweep strips such a park and the aeon's brief tells it not
#          to apply one, but the fact both of those read is this column.
#   base   the ref every branch in that repository is cut from and judged against, written as
#          git resolves it — `origin/main`, `origin/master`, a differently-named remote, or a
#          bare local branch for a repository nothing pushes. DO NOT ASSUME `main`: on the box
#          this harness was written for, three of seven repositories had no ref named `main`
#          at all and one had its remote named something other than `origin`. It is declared
#          here rather than derived because every automatic source is a local cache —
#          `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` is written at clone time and can be stale or absent, and
#          the checkout's own HEAD answers whatever branch a human last looked at, which was a
#          detached HEAD in two checkouts and a topic branch in two more. Leave it EMPTY to
#          let `spira_landref` resolve it; a repository whose answer cannot be established is
#          refused, never defaulted, because `main` is a guess and a guess here rebases
#          finished work onto a branch nobody chose.
#   format the repository's own formatter, run in the branch's tree after a rebase actually
#          replays commits, with the result committed onto the branch. EMPTY MEANS DO
#          NOTHING: a formatter the repository has not asked for is a thousand-line diff
#          nobody requested. It must be the WRITING form of the gate's checking form —
#          `cargo fmt --all` against `cargo fmt --all -- --check` — or the gate goes on
#          rejecting what the formatter just wrote. One field: it may not contain a pipe.
#   gate   a command run against a tree extracted from the BRANCH, in a minimal environment,
#          after the universal `bash -n` pass. Empty means syntax only. Keep it cheap and
#          cache-free: the tree is a fresh extraction, so anything that needs a warm build
#          cache will not be measuring what you think it is
#          (law-seen-red-must-be-seen-in-ci). Under `pr`, CI is the real gate and this is
#          pre-flight. May contain a pipe.
#   lanes  the persona lanes this repository admits. OPTIONAL: omitting it, or leaving it
#          empty (trailing pipe, nothing after it), yields `plan` alone. A row may name a
#          MODE or list lanes explicitly:
#
#            Modes (shorthand):
#              consume  plan
#              develop  plan, incident, groom, spike
#              self     develop + maechen-sweep, czar-trigger
#
#            Explicit list: comma-separated lane labels from the set
#              plan, incident, groom, maechen-sweep, spike, czar-trigger
#
#          An unknown mode name or unknown lane label is a hard parse error naming the row —
#          a typo must not quietly disable a lane. The accessor is spira_repo_lanes <name>.
#          This column is the GRANT side only; the project proposal (.spira/modes) and
#          SPIRA_FAYTHS are intersected with it by a later bead.
#          NOTE: a gate command that ends with a bare single-word token (e.g., `|| exit`)
#          is ambiguous; use `|| exit "$?"` or `|| { exit $?; }` instead.
#
# EVERY LINE CARRIES ALL SIX COLUMNS, EVEN WHERE THREE OF THEM ARE EMPTY; the seventh
# (lanes) is optional. A row written in an older, narrower form reads its GATE as a
# formatter and runs it in the branch's tree — so `test -f branch-only.txt` becomes
# something this harness executes and commits the result of.
# doctor.sh refuses a line with fewer than six fields for that reason.
#
# A `repo:` value ABSENT from this file is refused rather than guessed. An unmapped repo must
# fail closed — a default of the home repository is how another repository's bead gets "fixed"
# in the wrong tree, and the aeon reports success: it committed, on a branch, naming its bead.

# THE HOME REPOSITORY. The name a bead means when it names none. It must match
# SPIRA_HOME_REPO in your spira.conf, which defaults to the basename of the checkout the
# harness is installed in.
home         | /srv/checkouts/home        | push | origin/main   |                 | .claude/spira/gate-home.sh

# A Rust repository that lands through pull requests and its own CI. The formatter is the
# writing form of the gate's checking form; declaring one without the other is how a gate
# goes on rejecting what the formatter just wrote.
service      | /srv/checkouts/service     | pr   | origin/master | cargo fmt --all | cargo fmt --all -- --check

# A repository with NEITHER a gate nor a formatter, on purpose. Declare neither when
# `cargo fmt --all -- --check` already exits non-zero against that repository's own base:
# the gate would then reject every branch for a condition the branch did not cause, and the
# formatter would rewrite the repository out from under whatever the branch was doing. A gate
# must fail on the branch's fault and nothing else.
legacy       | /srv/checkouts/legacy      | pr   | origin/master |                 |

# A repository this harness may work in but must not advance: it gates the branch, notes the
# bead once, and leaves the merge to a human. Note the remote that is not named `origin` —
# the remote-tracking ref is still the right base, because "landed" means "reached what
# everything else pulls from".
vendor       | /srv/checkouts/vendor      | hold | upstream/master |               |

# A push repository whose gate has multiple fast fences before an expensive suite runner.
# ORDER MATTERS. Fast checks (fences) run first — no container started, no flock
# contended — and fail immediately when they find a violation. The suite runner follows
# only if the fences pass. All files named in the gate command must exist on the base
# before you wire them in; gate.sh returns NO_VERDICT until they do, holding every
# branch (law-seen-red-must-be-seen-in-ci). The exit-code mapping translates harness
# faults (container start failure → 2; configure failure → 3) to NO_VERDICT (75) so
# the bead is not charged an attempt for the harness's own failure.
platform     | /srv/checkouts/platform    | push | origin/main   |                 | bash .claude/fence-a.sh && bash .claude/fence-b.sh && { _b=0; bash .claude/testenv-batch.sh "$SPIRA_GATE_BRANCH" || _b=$?; case "$_b" in 2|3) exit 75;; *) exit "$_b";; esac; }

# A queue-mode repository. Branches are individually certified by their gate, then
# batched by batch.sh into one pull request. CI runs the full corpus on the batch PR
# and fast-forwards it to the base branch on green. Every landing cuts a release.
# Run `queue.sh protect <name>` once after switching to set the required check,
# disallow force-push, and disallow deletion on the base branch — doctor.sh warns
# for any queue-mode repo whose base branch has no protection record.
harness      | /srv/checkouts/harness     | queue | origin/main  |                 | bash spira/inventory.sh && bash spira/literal-lint.sh && bash spira/testdb-mode-lint.sh && bash spira/scratch-fence.sh && { _b=0; bash spira/testenv-batch.sh "$SPIRA_GATE_BRANCH" || _b=$?; case "$_b" in 2|3) exit 75;; *) exit "$_b";; esac; }
